= OODA loop =

{{:OODA loop/Simple}}

== Concept and provenance ==
The '''OODA loop''' ('''observe–orient–decide–act''') is a framework associated with US Air Force colonel John R. Boyd for understanding adaptive decisions and interactions in changing environments. Boyd's final 28 June 1995 ''The Essence of Winning and Losing'' diagram places '''orientation''' at the center of observation, choice and action, with feedback and implicit guidance—not four obligatory serial steps.<ref name="Boyd1995">John R. Boyd, ''The Essence of Winning and Losing'', 28 June 1995, reproduced in ''A Discourse on Winning and Losing'', Air University Press, 2018, appendix, pp. 383–386. [https://media.defense.gov/2018/May/22/2001920668/-1/-1/0/B_0151_Boyd_Discourse_Winning_Losing.pdf Full text].</ref>

== Interpretation ==
* '''Observe:''' gather signals from the situation and the effects of prior action.
* '''Orient:''' interpret signals using experience, culture, existing models and analysis; reconsider inadequate assumptions.
* '''Decide:''' choose a hypothesis or course of action when explicit choice is needed.
* '''Act:''' test the choice in the environment and attend to resulting feedback.

These labels are a teaching shorthand. Boyd's diagram also shows direct implicit guidance from orientation to observation and action. Thus it should not be represented as a mandatory four-stage algorithm.<ref name="Boyd1995"/>

== Uses and limits ==
Boyd developed the framework in strategic and military contexts; US Marine Corps tactical doctrine describes competitive decision cycles.<ref name="MCDP13">US Marine Corps, ''MCDP 1-3: Tactics'', chapter on decision cycles. [https://www.marines.mil/portals/1/publications/mcdp%201-3%20tactics.pdf Official publication].</ref> Business, incident response and organizational applications are analogies or adaptations, not proof that the framework improves outcomes in every setting. Speed without adequate orientation can amplify errors. The model supplies neither a validated universal timing formula nor a tested psychological stage theory.

